@dataclass(eq=False, repr=False)
class Event(betterproto.Message):
    """
    The per-event envelope. Common identity + exactly one typed body. 07 §2.
    """

    event_id: bytes = betterproto.bytes_field(1)
    """
    16-byte UUIDv7, RFC 9562 Method 1: a monotonic counter per daemon
     guarantees strict ordering even for same-millisecond bursts.
    
     This one 16-byte value carries four roles at once and the design only
     works while they stay the same bytes (07 §2): server dedupe key at
     controld.ingest, ClickHouse ReplacingMergeTree ordering-key tail, spool
     cursor (the leading 16 bytes of every spool record_id, 07 §6), and Merkle
     leaf identity in ReceiptCheckpoint. Splitting any role off breaks billing
     dedupe, merge collapse, ack-resume, or the offline receipt verifier.
    """

    seq: int = betterproto.uint64_field(2)
    """
    Per-session monotonic sequence assigned by mark1d at admission. This is
     the causal-order source of truth: developer clocks drift, so wall-clock
     ordering across events is not trustworthy; seq is.
    
     seq is unique only WITHIN a session_epoch — see session_epoch below.
    """

    session_id: bytes = betterproto.bytes_field(3)
    """
    16-byte session UUIDv7; duplicates Batch.session_id so events remain
     self-describing after the batch envelope is shed at ingest.
    """

    task_id: bytes = betterproto.bytes_field(4)
    """
    16-byte task UUIDv7 assigned at TaskOpen under task_seg.v1 rules;
     empty for events that precede or outlive any task.
    """

    occurred_at: datetime = betterproto.message_field(5)
    """
    Client wall clock at the instant the event occurred. Display and
     skew-analysis only — never ordering, never billing windows.
    """

    session_epoch: int = betterproto.uint32_field(6)
    """
    Daemon-boot epoch qualifying `seq`. Assigned by mark1d at boot from a
     durable, per-device, monotonically increasing counter; constant for the
     lifetime of one mark1d process.
    
     07 §2 is authoritative for this field and its ordering rule. The
     per-session seq counter
     lives in mark1d's memory, but sessions outlive the daemon (04 §11.4 calls
     launchd killing mark1d during sleep "not an edge case but a routine") and
     fully-acked spool segments are deleted whole (07 §6), so the seq
     high-water mark is not reconstructible from disk. A restarted daemon
     would otherwise re-issue seq 1, 2, 3… for a still-live session, producing
     duplicate (session_id, seq) pairs, and spill-ring replay would drain
     pre-crash events AFTER post-restart ones — inverting causal order in
     exactly the window where ordering matters most.
    
     ORDERING RULE, NORMATIVE: within one session_id, causal order is the
     LEXICOGRAPHIC order of the pair (session_epoch, seq) — compare
     session_epoch first, seq only to break ties. Every consumer that 07 has
     reading `seq` for order reads the pair instead: the §2 "causal-order
     source of truth", the §6/§7 replay and dedupe paths, task_seg.v1
     denominators, and attempt-indexed knee fitting. (session_id,
     session_epoch, seq) is unique; (session_id, seq) alone is not, across a
     restart.
    
     It rides the Event and not the Batch because a single batch legitimately
     mixes epochs: after a restart the uploader drains pre-restart spool
     records alongside freshly admitted ones.
    
     uint32, not uint64: it counts daemon boots on ONE device. 2^32 boots is
     ~136 years at one boot per second, the varint costs the same bytes as a
     uint64 at realistic values, and the narrower type keeps the field from
     reading like a timestamp. It is not a clock — it is never compared across
     devices and never interpreted as time.
    """

    session_start: "SessionStart" = betterproto.message_field(10, group="body")
    """events.proto families (10–29)"""

    session_end: "SessionEnd" = betterproto.message_field(11, group="body")
    task_open: "TaskOpen" = betterproto.message_field(12, group="body")
    task_close: "TaskClose" = betterproto.message_field(13, group="body")
    turn: "Turn" = betterproto.message_field(14, group="body")
    tool_use: "ToolUse" = betterproto.message_field(15, group="body")
    model_request: "ModelRequest" = betterproto.message_field(16, group="body")
    model_response: "ModelResponse" = betterproto.message_field(17, group="body")
